 > [!NOTE]
 > The `secure: true` option tells the browser to only store and send the cookie over HTTPS. When developing locally over plain HTTP, compliant browsers (notably Safari and iOS, and Chrome on some local domains) silently drop the cookie, so the session will not persist. Set `cookie: { secure: false }` during local development to work around this.
 
+### Previous passwords
+
+`extraUnsealPasswords` is an optional list of previous passwords. They are only used to open sessions: H3 tries `password` first, then each entry in array order, and the first one that verifies is used. Sessions are always sealed with `password`, so the list is how you change the password without logging everyone out. Each entry must be at least as long as the minimum for `password` (32 characters by default). An empty or shorter entry makes `useSession` and `getSession` throw an error that names `extraUnsealPasswords`, instead of being ignored.
+
+```js
+const session = await useSession(event, {
+  password: "new-password-generated-with-a-csprng-at-least-32-chars",
+  extraUnsealPasswords: ["old-password-generated-with-a-csprng-at-least-32-chars"],
+});
+```
+
+A session read from a cookie and opened with an entry from the list is resealed with `password` on the same request, so the cookies move to the new password as users browse. Sessions read from the `x-{name}-session` header cannot be resealed, so they keep opening with the old password until they expire. Keep the list short: every entry that does not match costs one more key derivation per request, and a forged cookie pays for all of them.
+
 ## Expiration
 
 Sessions have two independent expiration controls, and you can use either or both:
@@ -187,6 +200,16 @@ Halve `idleTimeout` if you need the shorter end of that range to be your real li
 
 The session cookie is also applied to error responses, so a request that throws still slides the window and still persists a session created during it.
 
+### Rotating the password
+
+To replace `password` without signing out users who are still logged in:
+
+1. Move the current `password` into `extraUnsealPasswords` and set the new password as `password`.
+2. Deploy. New and resealed cookies use the new password, and cookies still sealed with the old one keep working.
+3. Remove the old password from `extraUnsealPasswords` once every cookie sealed with it has expired. With `maxAge` set, that takes at most `maxAge` after the deploy. Without `maxAge` or `idleTimeout`, a sealed cookie never expires, so the old password can never be safely removed: set one of them first. Sessions read only from the header (`x-{name}-session`) do not move to the new password, so they expire on their own schedule.
+
+Removing the old password early signs out the users whose cookies still use it, and keeping it longer than needed leaves those cookies valid for longer.
+
 ## Use Multiple Sessions
 
 Because each session is stored under its own `name`, you can run several independent sessions on the same request. They live in separate cookies and never overwrite each other, which is useful for keeping unrelated concerns apart, such as a long-lived auth session and a short-lived flash message:
